{
  "results": [
    {
      "n": "G1",
      "evidence": "Screenshots 1 to 5 show the 3D scene rendering properly in Chrome with no blank screen or visual errors.",
      "pass": true
    },
    {
      "n": "G2",
      "evidence": "Screenshots 2 to 5 demonstrate camera orbiting and zooming, while OrbitControls in the code sets maxPolarAngle to Math.PI * 0.492 and maxDistance to 26 to keep the camera above the water and inside the sky dome.",
      "pass": true
    },
    {
      "n": "G3",
      "evidence": "The scene uses very lightweight procedural geometry (low-poly tubes, cones, and cylinders), a single 2048 shadow map, a 1024x1024 planar water reflection, and no post-processing, which will easily run above 30 fps on a standard laptop.",
      "pass": true
    },
    {
      "n": "1",
      "evidence": "Screenshots 1 to 5 display an on-screen UI text pill at the bottom ('Drag to Orbit 3D • Scroll to Zoom • Right-Drag to Pan'), and both zooming and panning are enabled in OrbitControls.",
      "pass": false
    },
    {
      "n": "2",
      "evidence": "In the animate() loop, water wave movement, subtle bird breathing, neck rotation, and foot ripple pulsing all run automatically on load without user input.",
      "pass": true
    },
    {
      "n": "3",
      "evidence": "Screenshot 1 frames the flamingo from a side view at the bird's height, centered and filling most of the vertical frame just like the reference photo.",
      "pass": true
    },
    {
      "n": "4",
      "evidence": "Screenshot 1 shows a single flamingo standing in calm water in the same mid-stride pose as the photo, with one straight vertical leg forward and the other bent backward.",
      "pass": true
    },
    {
      "n": "5",
      "evidence": "Screenshot 1 matches the reference palette with a coral-orange neck and body, pink legs with darker red joints, black flight feathers, and a pale upper beak with an orange middle and black tip.",
      "pass": true
    },
    {
      "n": "6",
      "evidence": "In Screenshots 1, 2, 3, and 5, the sky renders as a dark cobalt blue against a much paler blue water plane, creating a stark, high-contrast horizon line instead of the soft, even blue of the photo.",
      "pass": false
    },
    {
      "n": "7",
      "evidence": "Screenshot 1 and sunLight.position.set(7.5, 6.5, 5.5) place bright directional sunlight coming from the front right, illuminating the chest and front of the neck as in the photo.",
      "pass": true
    },
    {
      "n": "8",
      "evidence": "Screenshot 1 clearly shows the neck forming an S-curve with the head bent over and the hooked beak pointing straight down.",
      "pass": true
    },
    {
      "n": "9",
      "evidence": "While the legs are long and thin with backward-bending joints, the feet are modeled as tiny simple cones (ConeGeometry) rather than webbed feet, with a pointed red spike sticking out of the water in Screenshot 1.",
      "pass": false
    },
    {
      "n": "10",
      "evidence": "In Screenshots 1 to 3, the wings sit tightly against the torso with thin black cone spikes protruding out the back rather than reading as a partly raised wing revealing layered flight feathers.",
      "pass": false
    },
    {
      "n": "11",
      "evidence": "In Screenshots 1 to 3, only the rear left foot has a flat dark ring mesh around it while the front right leg enters the water with no ripple ring at all, and the left foot cone pokes awkwardly out of the surface.",
      "pass": false
    },
    {
      "n": "12",
      "evidence": "In Screenshot 1, because bodyMesh is shifted up by 0.31 units while neckMesh is not, the uncapped base of the neck tube juts out below the chest with a sharp disconnected rim.",
      "pass": false
    },
    {
      "n": "13",
      "evidence": "Screenshots 1, 2, and 3 show smooth directional shading across the curved neck and torso, with a bright sunlit front-right side and a softer sky-filled shaded side.",
      "pass": true
    },
    {
      "n": "14",
      "evidence": "In Screenshot 1, ACESFilmicToneMapping and hemisphere fill lighting keep highlights on the pale beak from blowing out to pure white and preserve subtle shading on dark areas.",
      "pass": true
    },
    {
      "n": "15",
      "evidence": "Screenshot 1 shows a crisp, hard edge along the horizon between the dark blue sky and light blue water rather than a soft, out-of-focus smooth gradient.",
      "pass": false
    },
    {
      "n": "16",
      "evidence": "Across Screenshots 1 to 4, the body, neck, and wings read as smooth plastic tubes and spiky geometric cones rather than soft, layered plumage.",
      "pass": false
    },
    {
      "n": "17",
      "evidence": "The beak is part of neckMesh and shares plumageMat (roughness 0.58 with a feather bump map), making the black beak tip appear dull matte in Screenshot 1 rather than glossy black.",
      "pass": false
    },
    {
      "n": "18",
      "evidence": "Screenshots 1 and 5 show a smooth, glossy water surface using Three.js Water with Fresnel reflections across the plane.",
      "pass": true
    },
    {
      "n": "19",
      "evidence": "Screenshot 1 shows the pink legs reflecting vertically in the water as in the photo's framing, and Screenshot 5 shows the full reflection of the bird and legs.",
      "pass": true
    },
    {
      "n": "20",
      "evidence": "Screenshots 1, 2, 3, and 5 show the water surface completely level across the frame with no unintended tilt.",
      "pass": true
    },
    {
      "n": "21",
      "evidence": "In the code, footRipple is a single MeshBasicMaterial ring on only one leg that slightly pulses in scale (1 + Math.sin(elapsed * 2.2) * 0.06) rather than spreading outward across the water or distorting the reflection.",
      "pass": false
    },
    {
      "n": "22",
      "evidence": "In animate(), flamingo.position.y oscillates gently for breathing and neckMesh.rotation.z adds a subtle continuous neck movement.",
      "pass": true
    },
    {
      "n": "23",
      "evidence": "Screenshots 1 to 5 and water.material.uniforms['time'].value = elapsed * 0.22 show gentle wind wavelets moving continuously across the water surface.",
      "pass": true
    },
    {
      "n": "24",
      "evidence": "All motions in animate() are driven by low-frequency sine waves and a seamlessly wrapping procedural normal map at elapsed * 0.22, running at calm real-world speed with no jumps.",
      "pass": true
    },
    {
      "n": "25",
      "evidence": "Screenshots 1 to 3 show only a single hard-edged semi-transparent ring around one foot rather than concentric rings fading with distance, and Screenshots 2, 3, and 5 reveal repeating horizontal banding patterns across the water.",
      "pass": false
    },
    {
      "n": "26",
      "evidence": "In Screenshot 1, the inverted white hip cones intersect abruptly into the underside of the torso and the left red foot cone pokes partway through the water surface like a spike.",
      "pass": false
    },
    {
      "n": "27",
      "evidence": "Screenshots 1 to 4 show smooth vertex-colored surfaces with a very subtle bump map and no visible UV texture seams or repeating tile patterns on the plumage.",
      "pass": true
    },
    {
      "n": "28",
      "evidence": "Screenshots 1 to 5 show clean antialiased 3D geometry edges against the sky and water with no cut-out jaggedness or alpha halos.",
      "pass": true
    },
    {
      "n": "29",
      "evidence": "Screenshot 1 clearly looks like a stylized geometric 3D model—with a disconnected neck base, spiky cone tail feathers, a sharp two-tone horizon, and a UI bar—and could not pass for the reference photo at first glance.",
      "pass": false
    },
    {
      "n": "30",
      "evidence": "In Screenshot 1, because the wing sits flush against the torso and wing meshes lack receiveShadow, there is only a thin, jagged shadow-acne sliver along the wing edge rather than soft shadows falling under a raised wing.",
      "pass": false
    }
  ]
}
